        title: "The bore sight",
        media: Loop(
            sheet: "self://training/combat_bore_sight.webp",
            columns: 4,
            rows: 5,
            frames: 20,
            frames_per_second: 10.0,
            alt: "a railgun\'s sight line arriving over the frame edge and crossing a patrol gunship, with a blue ring on a section the shot would destroy, and the ring walking the length of the hull as the gun is aimed off the spine and back",
        ),
        body: "The bore sight shows the railgun\'s path through its target. A ring marks each section the shot would destroy, and the line becomes thicker while the railgun charges.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/sections/railgun#the-bore-sight",
        field_notes: [
            "The bore sight marks every section the railgun shot would destroy.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "combat_collapse",
        category: Combat,
        order: 90,
        title: "When a ship comes apart",
        media: Loop(
            sheet: "self://training/combat_collapse.webp",
            columns: 4,
            rows: 5,
            frames: 20,
            frames_per_second: 10.0,
            alt: "one siege railgun shell boring a corridor through a block of reinforced hull, and the block then letting go of every section it has left and opening into a drifting cloud of plates",
        ),
        body: "A ship collapses when less than one twentieth of its original structure remains. A ship is neutralized when it loses every weapon or its last flight computer.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/ships#taking-a-ship-apart",
        practice: Some("drill_gunnery"),
        field_notes: [
            "A ship collapses when less than one twentieth of its original structure remains.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "build_sections",
        category: Shipbuilding,
        order: 10,
        title: "Ships are built from sections",
        media: Image(
            image: "self://training/build_sections.webp",
            alt: "the editor with one section selected on the hull it mates to",
        ),
        body: "Ships are built from sections connected socket to socket. These sockets are the only structural links between sections. The build grid uses cells that are 10 meters on each side.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/sections#what-every-section-shares",
        field_notes: [
            "Sections connect socket to socket.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "build_mass",
        category: Shipbuilding,
        order: 20,
        title: "Mass and thrust",
        media: Image(
            image: "self://training/build_mass.webp",
            alt: "the same hull with a heavier loadout, its numbers beside it",
        ),
        body: "Every section adds mass that your thrusters must move and stop. With the same thrusters, a heavier ship accelerates more slowly. Adding more thrusters can increase its acceleration.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/sections/thruster",
        field_notes: [
            "Heavier ships need more thrust to accelerate at the same rate.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "build_readout",
        category: Shipbuilding,
        order: 25,
        title: "The build readout",
        media: Image(
            image: "self://training/build_readout.webp",
            alt: "the editor rail\'s stat block on a half-built hull: Turn, Mass, Thrust, HP and Parts down one column with the limit note under them",
        ),
        body: "The editor shows your ship\'s turn rate, mass, thrust, health, and section count. A note below these values explains what currently limits its turn rate.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/keybinds#the-build-readout",
        field_notes: [
            "The build readout shows what limits your ship\'s turn rate.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "build_balance",
        category: Shipbuilding,
        order: 30,
        title: "Thruster placement",
        media: Image(
            image: "self://training/build_balance.webp",
            alt: "two thrusters on a lopsided hull in the editor, with the ship\'s turn and thrust figures beside it",
        ),
        body: "The flight computer balances your thrusters to prevent them from spinning the ship. A lopsided or damaged ship can still fly straight, but it may have less available thrust.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/sections/thruster",
        field_notes: [
            "The flight computer balances thrusters to keep your ship flying straight.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "build_turning",
        category: Shipbuilding,
        order: 35,
        title: "What decides your turn rate",
        media: Image(
            image: "self://training/build_turning.webp",
            alt: "a long spine in the editor, the rail printing its turn ceiling with the structure-limited note under it",
        ),
        body: "Turn rate is limited by your flight computers, the ship\'s mass, and the distance of its outermost sections. Long or heavy ships usually turn more slowly.",
        wiki_path: "wiki/sections/controller#what-sets-how-hard-a-ship-turns",
        field_notes: [
            "Long or heavy ships usually turn more slowly.",
        ],
    )),
    Lesson((
        id: "build_flight_test",
        category: Shipbuilding,
        order: 40,
        title: "Fly what you built",
        media: Loop(
            sheet: "self://training/build_flight_test.webp",
